    "question": {
      ":question/id": "424e6e1c-5295-51f8-93fa-ce62430d28b7",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2 t^{2} - 6 t - 1,\\,y=2 t^{2} + 2 t - 1,\\,t\\le 0,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\nx=2 t^{2} - 6 t - 1=2 \\left(t - \\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{2} - \\frac{11}{2} .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=\\frac{3}{2}$, which is outside the given domain. On the given half-line the endpoint value is $-1$, and the endpoint is included. The coordinate is unbounded above. Consequently, $x\\ge -1.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\ny=2 t^{2} + 2 t - 1=2 \\left(t + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2} - \\frac{3}{2} .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=- \\frac{1}{2}$, which is admissible. The vertex value is $- \\frac{3}{2}$ and is attained. The coordinate is unbounded above. Consequently, $y\\ge - \\frac{3}{2}.$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
