      ":question/id": "9fc14b54-5538-5c05-b649-82213e5b7c35",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} - 2 t + 4,\\,y=2 t^{2} + 6 t + 4,\\,t>-3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} - 2 t + 4=5 - \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=-1$, which is admissible. The vertex value is $5$ and is attained. The coordinate is unbounded below. Consequently, $x\\le 5.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ complete the square:\n\n$$\ny=2 t^{2} + 6 t + 4=2 \\left(t + \\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} .\n$$\n\nThe vertex parameter is $t=- \\frac{3}{2}$, which is admissible. The vertex value is $- \\frac{1}{2}$ and is attained. The coordinate is unbounded above. Consequently, $y\\ge - \\frac{1}{2}.$",
